{"id":"30106536-fe11-4c95-b08c-90055780660e", "recipient":"fe679799-378f-4b64-973a-d55f622dfbba", "sender":"4bbdc7b5-209f-4b1c-adf5-ac8fe6f627cd", "priority":"MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH", "timestamp":"2026-09-22T20:26:40.379719100Z", "renderDetails":{"messageTitle":"Message from Beta Tester (browser)"}, "content":"Connection Error: Unable to connect to Chrome via Chrome DevTools MCP.\n\nThe tool returned the following error:\n`Could not connect to Chrome. Check if Chrome is running. Cause: Could not find DevToolsActivePort for chrome at C:\\Users\\samsung\\AppData\\Local\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\DevToolsActivePort`\n\nIn accordance with Chrome connection & remote debugging guidelines, execution has been stopped immediately. Please perform the following troubleshooting steps:\n1. Check the DevToolsActivePort file of the user's default Chrome profile at `C:\\Users\\samsung\\AppData\\Local\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\DevToolsActivePort`.\n   - Ensure Chrome is running with remote debugging enabled on the default user profile (e.g., standard Chrome instance or `chrome.exe --remote-debugging-port=9222` without a detached/isolated user-data-dir that prevents DevToolsActivePort from being created in the default directory).\n2. Check that port 9222 is open and listening.\n3. Verify if there are multiple processes listening on port 9222, and kill any conflicting or orphaned processes.\n4. Ensure remote debugging is enabled in Chrome (or navigate to `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging` to verify settings).\n\nOnce Chrome is running with remote debugging active on the default profile, please re-invoke to proceed with the beta testing session.\nNote: The recording of this session is available in the conversation artifacts.", "sourceMetadata":{"tool":{"conversationId":"4bbdc7b5-209f-4b1c-adf5-ac8fe6f627cd", "stepIndex":12, "toolCall":{"id":"call_44062", "name":"send_message", "argumentsJson":"{\"Message\":\"Connection Error: Unable to connect to Chrome via Chrome DevTools MCP.\\n\\nThe tool returned the following error:\\n`Could not connect to Chrome. Check if Chrome is running. Cause: Could not find DevToolsActivePort for chrome at C:\\\\Users\\\\samsung\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Google\\\\Chrome\\\\User Data\\\\DevToolsActivePort`\\n\\nIn accordance with Chrome connection \\u0026 remote debugging guidelines, execution has been stopped immediately. Please perform the following troubleshooting steps:\\n1. Check the DevToolsActivePort file of the user's default Chrome profile at `C:\\\\Users\\\\samsung\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Google\\\\Chrome\\\\User Data\\\\DevToolsActivePort`.\\n   - Ensure Chrome is running with remote debugging enabled on the default user profile (e.g., standard Chrome instance or `chrome.exe --remote-debugging-port=9222` without a detached/isolated user-data-dir that prevents DevToolsActivePort from being created in the default directory).\\n2. Check that port 9222 is open and listening.\\n3. Verify if there are multiple processes listening on port 9222, and kill any conflicting or orphaned processes.\\n4. Ensure remote debugging is enabled in Chrome (or navigate to `chrome://inspect/#remote-debugging` to verify settings).\\n\\nOnce Chrome is running with remote debugging active on the default profile, please re-invoke to proceed with the beta testing session.\\nNote: The recording of this session is available in the conversation artifacts.\",\"Recipient\":\"fe679799-378f-4b64-973a-d55f622dfbba\",\"toolAction\":\"Reporting Chrome connection failure\",\"toolSummary\":\"Report Chrome connection failure\"}", "thinkingSignature":""}}}}